WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:04.504 line:0%
-Behra pulls up next
to you, challenging.

00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:06.105 line:0%
You're even.

00:00:06.105 --> 00:00:08.875 line:0%
But two objects cannot occupy
the same point in space

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:10.876 line:0%
at the same moment in time.

00:00:10.876 --> 00:00:15.815 line:0%
Behra doesn't lift.
The corner races at you.

00:00:15.815 --> 00:00:18.217 line:0%
You have perhaps
a crisis of identity.

00:00:18.217 --> 00:00:21.020 line:0%
Am I a sportsman
or a competitor?

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:24.256 line:0%
How will the French think of me
if I run Behra into a tree?

00:00:24.256 --> 00:00:26.526 line:0%
You lift, he passes.

00:00:26.526 --> 00:00:28.795
He won, you lose.

00:00:30.896 --> 00:00:32.732
-We're back with Adam Driver,
everybody.

00:00:32.732 --> 00:00:33.866
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:33.866 --> 00:00:35.534
That was a clip from "Ferrari."

00:00:35.534 --> 00:00:39.204
I feel like
when I was a kid and, like,

00:00:39.204 --> 00:00:41.274
went through an era where I was
obsessed with things

00:00:41.274 --> 00:00:44.244
like Ferraris and cars,
it never connected with me

00:00:44.244 --> 00:00:46.412
that, of course, Ferrari
was somebody's last name,

00:00:46.412 --> 00:00:48.781
who just, like, had the idea
to make cars like this.

00:00:48.781 --> 00:00:50.349
Did you know much
about Enzo Ferrari

00:00:50.349 --> 00:00:51.384
before you started doing this?

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:53.653
-No. Oddly the only --
my first --

00:00:53.653 --> 00:00:55.221
I think in retrospect,
looking back,

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:58.824
that my first intro to Ferraris
was from Michael's

00:00:58.824 --> 00:01:01.393
"Miami Vice" because my dad
was a big "Miami Vice" fan.

00:01:01.393 --> 00:01:04.897
Of course, he has the Ferrari
in that, that he blew up,

00:01:04.897 --> 00:01:10.070
and that was kind of his intro
to Enzo at the time.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:12.605
They had
I think a phone correspondence.

00:01:12.605 --> 00:01:15.107
They may have met one time,
but they blew up a replica car

00:01:15.107 --> 00:01:16.442
and he's like, "Why don't you
have the real thing?"

00:01:16.442 --> 00:01:21.481
So, anyway, but I knew of them
as drivable works of art.

00:01:21.481 --> 00:01:23.917
But, you know,
growing up in Indiana,

00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:26.752
there was -- or just anywhere,
they were out of my price range.

00:01:26.752 --> 00:01:27.954
-Right.
-Yeah.

00:01:27.954 --> 00:01:29.222
-You wouldn't, like, go out to
the main road

00:01:29.222 --> 00:01:31.223
and say like, "Let's see
if we see a Ferrari today."

00:01:31.223 --> 00:01:33.126
-Yeah, yeah.
Show me the 355.

00:01:33.126 --> 00:01:35.995
-Yeah. Did you --
And I did not realize

00:01:35.995 --> 00:01:38.698
that he basically --
he wanted to race cars,

00:01:38.698 --> 00:01:41.868
and he only sold them
to sort of fund the racing.

00:01:41.868 --> 00:01:43.269
-Yeah, he was a racer first.

00:01:43.269 --> 00:01:46.305
And at this point
that we find him in 1957,

00:01:46.305 --> 00:01:49.875
in this three-month period,
he was going bankrupt

00:01:49.875 --> 00:01:51.777
because he was more interested
in racing cars

00:01:51.777 --> 00:01:54.347
and kind of almost resented
the customer part of being,

00:01:54.347 --> 00:01:56.549
you know,
running a car company

00:01:56.549 --> 00:01:59.152
and, you know, was spending
all of his money on that

00:01:59.152 --> 00:02:00.719
and not so much
commercial vehicles.

00:02:00.719 --> 00:02:03.155
And also the game was changing.

00:02:03.155 --> 00:02:06.058
It was -- TV was becoming
more a part of racing.

00:02:06.058 --> 00:02:08.928
So he was more incentivized to,
you know, you win on Sunday,

00:02:08.928 --> 00:02:10.262
you sell cars on Monday.

00:02:10.262 --> 00:02:12.098
-Must not have been
a terrible place.

00:02:12.098 --> 00:02:13.599
Obviously when you travel
for a film

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:15.034
and you're away
from your family,

00:02:15.034 --> 00:02:17.903
not ideal, but being in Italy
must have been outstanding.

00:02:17.903 --> 00:02:20.206
-Yeah, being away anywhere
without my family

00:02:20.206 --> 00:02:22.942
is outstanding
but especially with Italy...

00:02:22.942 --> 00:02:23.943
[ Laughter ]

00:02:23.943 --> 00:02:25.978
Actually, my family
was with me.

00:02:25.978 --> 00:02:27.646
-Oh, good.
-So it was...

00:02:27.646 --> 00:02:29.415
-Win-win. Or win-lose.
-Yeah.

00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:31.217
Everyone's like, "You do
a lot of movies in Italy."

00:02:31.217 --> 00:02:32.285
I'm like, "Isn't it obvious?"

00:02:32.285 --> 00:02:33.886
-Yeah, yeah, yeah,
that's very smart.

00:02:33.886 --> 00:02:36.589
-Yeah.
-Even the clip we showed,

00:02:36.589 --> 00:02:38.591
one of many scenes
at a dinner table,

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:40.560
which feels very Italian...

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.028
-Yeah.
-And I would imagine

00:02:43.028 --> 00:02:44.697
they want to get
the authenticity of the food

00:02:44.697 --> 00:02:46.832
when you're doing those
dinner table scenes.

00:02:46.832 --> 00:02:48.334
-Oh, I didn't think about that.

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:49.736
But yeah.

00:02:49.736 --> 00:02:51.404
-Like, if there was, like,
just, like a big thing

00:02:51.404 --> 00:02:52.739
of Wonder Bread, you'd be like,
"What are they doing?"

00:02:52.739 --> 00:02:54.774
-Yeah, yeah. I would know
that something is wrong.

00:02:54.774 --> 00:02:59.512
Yeah, but Michael is very,
food aside,

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:03.883
he is relentless about
authenticity in his films.

00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:06.652
In this film, the barbershop
where he goes

00:03:06.652 --> 00:03:08.153
is the barbershop he went to.

00:03:08.153 --> 00:03:09.555
The guy who was cutting my hair

00:03:09.555 --> 00:03:12.658
is the grandson of the guy
who actually cut Enzo's hair.

00:03:12.658 --> 00:03:16.029
You know, he brought in replica

00:03:16.029 --> 00:03:20.099
or the real Maseratis
and some Testarossa.

00:03:20.099 --> 00:03:22.601
They would do scans
of the ones

00:03:22.601 --> 00:03:25.204
that were historical vehicles
and duplicate them.

00:03:25.204 --> 00:03:26.405
The ones that he did
bring in that

00:03:26.405 --> 00:03:28.941
were like $70 million Ferraris.

00:03:28.941 --> 00:03:31.310
He would attach seven mikes to
them and drive them in tunnels

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:33.012
because you can't
replicate the sound.

00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:34.414
It has to be in the place,
you know.

00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:36.816
He's relentless.

00:03:36.816 --> 00:03:38.518
So I have no doubt that
Wonder Bread

00:03:38.518 --> 00:03:40.119
was not part of the dinner.

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:41.587
-It'd be really funny
if they were like,

00:03:41.587 --> 00:03:43.989
"We have to use Wonder Bread
because you made us drive

00:03:43.989 --> 00:03:45.992
a $70 million car."
-Right, right.

00:03:45.992 --> 00:03:48.628
-"We don't have money left
for good bread, Michael."

00:03:48.628 --> 00:03:51.030
It's a wonderful film.
I should note

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:52.398
the rest of the cast
is great as well.

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:53.966
And it's always such a delight
to see you, Adam.

00:03:53.966 --> 00:03:55.100
-Thank you so much
for having me.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.437
-Happy holidays
to you and your family.

00:03:57.437 --> 00:04:00.606
Adam Driver. "Ferrari," theaters
on Christmas day.

00:04:00.606 --> 00:04:02.208
Stick around. We'll be
right back with Gary Gulman.

